    "title": "Chapter 12 — Measure the Business, Not Just the Launch",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Chapter 12 — Measure the business, not just the launch\nA launch can generate attention without generating a sustainable business. Track the complete customer journey and the economics of the partnership.",
      "Core metrics\n| Metric | What it tells you |\n| --- | --- |\n| Reach | How many people were exposed to the launch |\n| Product-page visits | How many people considered the offer |\n| Checkout starts | How many showed stronger purchase intent |\n| Purchases | Number of completed orders |\n| Conversion rate | Purchases divided by the relevant visitor count |\n| Average order value | Average collected value per order |\n| Refund rate | Share of orders refunded |\n| Chargeback rate | Share of payments disputed |\n| Net collected revenue | Revenue after refunds and agreed deductions |\n| Operator share | Amount due to the operator under the agreement |\n| Support volume | Work required after purchase |\n| Completion or usage | Whether buyers are actually using the product, if measurable |",
      "Use consistent definitions. For example, page conversion rate might be purchases divided by unique product-page visitors, while checkout conversion might be purchases divided by checkout starts. Do not mix them.",
      "Basic revenue model\nLet:\n- \\(F\\) = relevant audience size.\n- \\(r\\) = proportion who visit the offer page.\n- \\(c\\) = purchase conversion rate among visitors.\n- \\(P\\) = product price.\n- \\(q\\) = proportion of gross sales retained after refunds, chargebacks, fees, and agreed deductions.\n- \\(s\\) = operator share of distributable revenue.\nThen:\n\\[\n\\text{Operator share} = F \\times r \\times c \\times P \\times q \\times s\n\\]\nThis model is more useful than multiplying followers directly by a purchase percentage because not every follower sees the offer page.",
      "The source’s $13.5k illustration, unpacked\n| Assumption | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Followers | 30,000 |\n| Assumed buyers | 1% of followers |\n| Buyers | 300 |\n| Product price | $150 |\n| Gross sales | $45,000 |\n| Operator share | 30% |\n| Operator gross share before costs | $13,500 |",
      "The calculation is mathematically correct under those assumptions. The assumptions are not validated by the transcript. In particular, 300 buyers from 30,000 followers is a demanding outcome that should never be presented as a normal or expected result.",
      "A more cautious scenario model\nThe following scenarios are hypothetical planning examples only. They are not industry benchmarks.\n| Scenario | Page visits | Purchase conversion | Price | Gross sales |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Small test | 300 | 2% | $49 | $294 |\n| Moderate test | 1,000 | 3% | $79 | $2,370 |\n| Strong test | 2,000 | 5% | $99 | $9,900 |",
      "At a 30% operator share, before fees and costs, these would produce $88.20, $711, and $2,970 respectively. The point is not to predict results; it is to understand how much traffic, conversion, and pricing influence revenue.",
      "Weekly review questions\n- Which content attracted relevant attention?\n- Which questions appeared repeatedly?\n- How many people visited the offer page?\n- Where did potential buyers leave?\n- What objections did they raise?\n- What did buyers misunderstand?\n- Were refunds or support requests unusually high?\n- Did the creator and operator complete their agreed responsibilities?\n- What one change should be tested next?\nChange one major variable at a time where possible. If you change the product, price, page, and launch message simultaneously, it becomes harder to understand what caused the result.\nPart 5 — Build a Sustainable Operator Business"
    ]
